  "terms": {
    "en": "Indefinite and negative pronouns",
    "es": "Pronombres indefinidos y negativos",
    "it": "Pronomi indefiniti e negativi",
    "fr": "Pronoms indéfinis et négatifs",
    "de": "Indefinit- und Negationspronomen",
    "ru": "Неопределённые и отрицательные местоимения",
    "hu": "Határozatlan és tagadó névmások",
    "pt": "Pronomes indefinidos e negativos",
    "ro": "Pronume nehotărâte și negative"
  },
  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "Indefinite and negative pronouns",
      "summary": "These pronouns refer to people or things without saying which, or say that there are none.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "An indefinite pronoun refers to a person or thing in a general way. Some, any, every and no combine with -one, -body, -thing and -where to form a family of compound pronouns. Negative pronouns, such as nobody and nothing, say that there is none."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**Somebody** is waiting for you.",
                "Do you need **anything**?",
                "**Nothing** happened."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>People</th><th>Things</th><th>Places</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>some-</strong></td><td>someone, somebody</td><td>something</td><td>somewhere</td></tr><tr><td><strong>any-</strong></td><td>anyone, anybody</td><td>anything</td><td>anywhere</td></tr><tr><td><strong>every-</strong></td><td>everyone, everybody</td><td>everything</td><td>everywhere</td></tr><tr><td><strong>no-</strong></td><td>no one, nobody</td><td>nothing</td><td>nowhere</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Some- words are used in positive sentences and offers: Would you like something to eat? Any- words are used in questions, negatives and with if: I don't know anyone here. No- words already contain the negative, so the verb must be positive."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Agreement",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Compound pronouns take a singular verb, even when they mean many people: Everyone is here. In informal English, the plural they, them and their refers back to them: Everyone brought their own lunch."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Double negatives",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Standard English does not allow two negatives in one clause. Use either not... anything or nothing, not both."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Not standard</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I didn't see anything.</strong></td><td>I didn't see nothing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I saw nothing.</strong></td><td>I didn't see nothing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>There isn't anybody here.</strong></td><td>There isn't nobody here.</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Other indefinite pronouns",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**one**: One should be honest. (formal and general)",
                "**each**, **either**, **neither**: Each of the boys has a bike.",
                "**both**, **many**, **several**, **few**: Several are missing.",
                "**other**, **another**, **others**: I like the other."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
